1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:05,691
MAYA: So Finn, I've done as much as I can for our project on theatre programmes.

2
00:00:05,691 --> 00:00:07,700
How's your research coming along?

3
00:00:07,700 --> 00:00:16,170
FINN: OK, Maya. I didn't know theatre programmes are called playbills in the USA till I started looking into the topic.

4
00:00:16,170 --> 00:00:19,300
FINN: Even though I struggled to find many useful websites

5
00:00:19,700 --> 00:00:21,340
I'm glad we picked this subject.

6
00:00:21,340 --> 00:00:24,990
No one else on the course is doing the same as us,

7
00:00:24,990 --> 00:00:28,640
although it is one of the research areas of the module convenor.

8
00:00:28,640 --> 00:00:30,940
MAYA: That might actually put some people off.

9
00:00:30,940 --> 00:00:33,087
FINN: I suppose so. Anyway,

10
00:00:33,087 --> 00:00:38,670
I hadn't realised there are actually companies specialising in creating theatre programmes.

11
00:00:38,670 --> 00:00:41,288
MAYA: Yes, they're quite common nowadays.

12
00:00:41,288 --> 00:00:46,897
Contrary to what many people think, theatres don't hire people to do the programmes,

13
00:00:46,897 --> 00:00:52,133
in fact companies buy the rights to publish programmes on the theatre's behalf -

14
00:00:52,133 --> 00:00:56,620
and then make their money selling advertising space within the programme booklet.

15
00:00:56,620 --> 00:00:59,330
FINN: It must be easier for theatres to do it that way.

16
00:00:59,330 --> 00:01:00,440
MAYA: Yes.

17
00:01:00,440 --> 00:01:04,675
FINN: I remember reading something about programmes in early British theatre.

18
00:01:04,675 --> 00:01:08,140
It said that the cast was always very important.

19
00:01:08,140 --> 00:01:12,040
MAYA: Yeah, audiences were very familiar with leading actors

20
00:01:12,040 --> 00:01:14,970
and big names would draw huge crowds.

21
00:01:14,970 --> 00:01:19,463
FINN: But I hadn't realised that if the programme named a famous actor,

22
00:01:19,463 --> 00:01:22,030
that's who the public expected to perform.

23
00:01:22,030 --> 00:01:23,640
FINN: And if that didn't happen

24
00:01:23,880 --> 00:01:27,711
people accused the theatre of breaking their agreement with the audience.

25
00:01:27,711 --> 00:01:32,130
They would demand refunds, and, if they didn't get them, there were riots!

26
00:01:32,130 --> 00:01:35,790
MAYA: Outrageous. That'd never happen now.

27
00:01:35,790 --> 00:01:37,940
FINN: No, people are too polite

28
00:01:38,220 --> 00:01:42,260
even when they're disappointed if the star of the show misses a performance.

29
00:01:42,260 --> 00:01:47,393
MAYA: We should definitely include that information about early audiences in our project.

30
00:01:47,393 --> 00:01:54,501
I also think it's important to mention that lots of ordinary people at that time were illiterate,

31
00:01:54,501 --> 00:01:58,450
so theatre programmes were of limited value in advertising plays.

32
00:01:58,450 --> 00:02:00,800
MAYA: When a company of actors arrived in a town

33
00:02:00,940 --> 00:02:05,232
they'd parade around the streets in their costumes,

34
00:02:05,232 --> 00:02:07,730
beating drums and announcing their upcoming performances.

35
00:02:07,730 --> 00:02:11,330
FINN: Interesting. I couldn't imagine that happening now either.

36
00:02:11,330 --> 00:02:18,650
MAYA: There's also an interesting comparison to make between eighteenth and nineteenth-century programmes.

37
00:02:18,650 --> 00:02:24,170
FINN: Wasn't it in the nineteenth century that theatre programmes started to resemble programmes today?

38
00:02:24,170 --> 00:02:30,360
MAYA: Yes. And, unlike programmes from the eighteenth century, they always used colour.

39
00:02:30,360 --> 00:02:34,516
FINN: And there was a greater variety of designs.

40
00:02:34,516 --> 00:02:38,209
But personally, I think eighteenth-century programmes were superior

41
00:02:38,209 --> 00:02:43,750
because they told the theatre-goers so many things, including about the actors.

42
00:02:43,750 --> 00:02:49,100
MAYA: And about the writer, the plot, and sometimes the history of the play.

43
00:02:49,100 --> 00:02:54,810
FINN: That's right.... What should we say about theatre programmes in the twentieth century?

44
00:02:54,810 --> 00:03:00,970
MAYA: I reckon the most important thing is the dramatic change they underwent during World War Two.

45
00:03:00,970 --> 00:03:04,380
FINN: When the government imposed restrictions on the use of paper.

46
00:03:04,380 --> 00:03:08,209
MAYA: Yeah, but that was only in the UK.

47
00:03:08,209 --> 00:03:11,540
In the USA, programmes - or rather, playbills

48
00:03:12,100 --> 00:03:14,590
continued to be published in the same format...

49
00:03:14,590 --> 00:03:16,779
FINN:... while here in the UK,

50
00:03:16,779 --> 00:03:22,250
programmes became merely a single sheet of paper folded to create four pages for text.

51
00:03:22,250 --> 00:03:26,465
MAYA: What I don't really get is that, after the war,

52
00:03:26,465 --> 00:03:29,160
they didn't go back to being more than one sheet

53
00:03:29,160 --> 00:03:33,490
or change in any way for over twenty-five years.

54
00:03:33,490 --> 00:03:36,120
MAYA: I know there were paper shortages after the war

55
00:03:36,220 --> 00:03:38,900
but only for five or ten years.

56
00:03:38,900 --> 00:03:40,550
FINN: Strange.

57
00:03:40,550 --> 00:03:45,570
MAYA: I've got some pictures of programmes we could include on the slides for our presentation.

58
00:03:45,570 --> 00:03:50,030
FINN: I found a couple too, Maya. Let's go through and see what we think.

59
00:03:50,030 --> 00:03:55,790
MAYA: Erm, this is an old one, for a play called Ruy Blas.

60
00:03:55,790 --> 00:04:00,210
FINN: Never heard of that, but the programme looks very decorative.

61
00:04:00,210 --> 00:04:02,660
MAYA: Good enough to put in a frame on the wall

62
00:04:03,120 --> 00:04:07,700
The images are just beautiful. Finn, what did you find?

63
00:04:07,700 --> 00:04:12,337
FINN: I've got some pages from a programme for Man of La Mancha.

64
00:04:12,337 --> 00:04:15,318
I thought this was a good programme to show,

65
00:04:15,318 --> 00:04:16,000
not because of the pictures

66
00:04:16,400 --> 00:04:20,950
but because it contains articles written by members of the theatre company.

67
00:04:20,950 --> 00:04:23,720
FINN: So we can learn how the production was created

68
00:04:23,720 --> 00:04:26,830
and the thoughts and feelings of the cast.

69
00:04:26,830 --> 00:04:31,100
MAYA: Good. I've got a copy of a programme that's now in a museum

70
00:04:31,560 --> 00:04:34,559
It's for The Tragedy of Jane Shore

71
00:04:34,559 --> 00:04:41,000
and it's said to be the earliest surviving document to have been printed on Australia's first printing press.

72
00:04:41,000 --> 00:04:42,910
FINN: Fantastic.

73
00:04:42,910 --> 00:04:46,912
MAYA: Another programme to talk about is for The Sailors' Festival.

74
00:04:46,912 --> 00:04:53,460
It comes from the British Library's digitised collection of programmes that was started a few years ago.

75
00:04:53,460 --> 00:04:59,380
MAYA: It already comprises over two hundred thousand programmes, which is amazing.

76
00:04:59,380 --> 00:05:04,000
FINN: Wish I'd known about it while I was doing my research!
